post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
smckbd is built by DKMS for every installed kernel 7.3 and newer. Older
kernels keep applesmc, which already provides smc::kbd_backlight.

smckbd.service (module load) is enabled but not started here: the DKMS hook may
not have built the module yet. Start it once, or reboot:
  sudo systemctl start smckbd.service

For the F5/F6 keys pick ONE of these:

  a) bind them in your session, no root needed (recommended):
       XF86KbdBrightnessUp   -> smckbd-keys up
       XF86KbdBrightnessDown -> smckbd-keys down
     Note XF86MonBrightnessUp/Down are the display keys - leave those alone.

  b) no key bindings? run the watcher instead, as root:
       sudo systemctl enable --now smckbd-keys.service
     Do not do both: a running daemon plus bindings steps the backlight twice.

The udev rule smckbd-led.rules gives the video group write access to the
backlight, so the bound commands need no root; make sure your user is in
"video".

Check it:
  ls -l /sys/class/leds/ | grep kbd_backlight
  echo 120 | sudo tee /sys/class/leds/smc::kbd_backlight/brightness
EOF

  if [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
    systemctl enable smckbd.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    udevadm control --reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=leds >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  if [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
    systemctl disable --now smckbd.service smckbd-keys.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
}
